        <h2 id="s2s-buckets">Shared, similar, different, missing, by layer</h2>
        <p class="section-lede">Every artifact from both inventories sits in exactly one row, and no row pairs across layers: {DEV_ROWS} rows in the developer layer, {PRODUCT_ROWS} in the in-product layer.</p>

        {BUCKETS.map((b) => (
          <article class="bucket" id={b.key}>
            <header class="bucket__head">
              <h3>{b.label}</h3>
              <span class="bucket__badge"><span class={`swatch swatch--${b.key}`} aria-hidden="true"></span>dev {b.dev} of {DEV_ROWS} · product {b.product} of {PRODUCT_ROWS}</span>
            </header>
            <div class="minibars" aria-hidden="true">
              <span class="minibar"><span class="minibar__k">dev</span><span class="meter"><span class={`meter__fill meter__fill--${b.key}`} style={`width:${(b.dev / DEV_ROWS) * 100}%`}></span></span></span>
              <span class="minibar"><span class="minibar__k">product</span><span class="meter"><span class={`meter__fill meter__fill--${b.key}`} style={`width:${(b.product / PRODUCT_ROWS) * 100}%`}></span></span></span>
            </div>
            {b.key === 'shared' && (
              <Fragment>
                <p class="bucket__summary">Both sides keep the same kind of artifact; the interest is in how differently each is written and kept up.</p>
                <h4>Developer layer</h4>
                <div class="prose">
                  <p><strong>Permission files.</strong> Both sides commit a Claude Code settings file, and neither treats it as a policy. Theirs is about 210 <code>allow</code> entries saved from click-approvals, committed once on 2026-08-20, granting push, deploy and credential commands; the permission allowlist held credentials. Ours are tiny but tracked in git, though <code>settings.local.json</code> is meant to stay on one machine. They got that right: their local file stayed local. Neither side has a <code>deny</code> list or a hook.</p>
                  <p><strong>Dev skills.</strong> Their <code>verify</code> skill is a lesson learned the hard way: <q><code>--only functions</code> alone leaves Firestore calls pointed at production</q>. Our <code>decile-hub-interface</code> is the same kind of guide for a system where a careless agent touches real data. Theirs sits where Claude Code finds it with no setup; ours are found only once linked, and at the snapshot none of augment-it's or memopop's project skills was. On discoverability, their simple path wins.</p>
                  <p><strong>Commit history</strong> is the closest match. 220 of their 263 non-merge commits carry a Claude trailer, with subjects that state findings (<q>Fix the check, not the briefs: five of eight preflight failures were mine</q>). They got lab-notebook commits without a written convention; we wrote <code>git-conventions</code> down so it holds across 27 repos.</p>
                </div>
                <h4>In-product layer</h4>
                <div class="prose">
                  <p><strong>Runtime prompt builders</strong> on both sides pin behaviour in comments next to the prompt. Their grader runs three passes and averages; our writer is pinned to temperature 0. Two answers to the same worry about variance: theirs measures it, ours suppresses it.</p>
                  <p><strong>Model routing</strong> sits behind one module on both sides; they keep the choice in runtime config, we keep it in code. <strong>Run records</strong> converge: the flight recorder and our artifact trail both write every step to disk. <q>Don't tune on reasoning alone. Get the flight-recorder data first.</q></p>
                  <p><strong>Tests of the LLM machinery</strong> exist on both sides. Their fixtures README, <q>exam-craft edits must never break platform tests</q>, marks the boundary between the two layers better than anything on our side.</p>
                </div>
              </Fragment>
            )}
            {b.key === 'similar' && (
              <Fragment>
                <p class="bucket__summary">Same job, different form: in the product layer, this is where most of the learning sits.</p>
                <h4>Developer layer</h4>
                <div class="prose">
                  <p><strong>Specs.</strong> Their dev layer is not spec-less: <q>Design doc for review. Nothing gets built until this is signed off.</q> The architecture doc has dated "what shipped" sections, a "Start here in a fresh session" section and epistemic tags. The discipline matches ours; the difference is structure. Their <code>docs/</code> is flat and unindexed, and nothing points an agent at it.</p>
                  <p><strong>Handoffs, browser drives, to-do lists.</strong> Their session opener carries platform to-dos but exists for exam sessions. Their browser drives run from the scratchpad and are thrown away; we codified ours in a blueprint and committed proof scripts. Both sides let to-do lists rot.</p>
                </div>
                <h4>In-product layer</h4>
                <div class="prose">
                  <p><strong>Runtime rulebooks.</strong> Their rulebook names itself the single source of truth (<q>revise this section, not individual exams</q>). The orchestrator's <code>AGENTS.md</code> has numbered principles that the code cites. Ours carries a naming hazard: in the tree, <code>AGENTS.md</code> also means coding-agent instructions.</p>
                  <p><strong>Craft ledgers.</strong> Their <code>EXAM_CRAFT.md</code> is one 2,163-line file: stable IDs, an evidence line per finding, a part for <q>candidate principles, not yet distilled</q>, an append-only build log, a checked index. Our product craft is spread across 36 issue-resolution and reminder files. Ours is easier to publish; theirs does the job a ledger exists for.</p>
                  <p><strong>Assemble, don't generate</strong>, the strongest convergence. They copy structure from a skeleton and validate invariants that <q>are silently violable</q>. The orchestrator fixes section taxonomy in an outline YAML with a schema because drift <q>made merging seven ChromaDB runs produce 26 best-of catalogs instead of 9</q>.</p>
                  <p><strong>Generation commands and human gates.</strong> <code>/exam-build</code> enforces its tool list and two stops (<q>Do not deploy, commit, or import anything into the builder. The user does that.</q>); our memo skills cover more situations but only describe their stops. Both sides gate generation on a human, at different points.</p>
                  <p><strong>Grading the output.</strong> Their audits check whether the model did what it was told; our five checking agents and scorers check whether the output is true and good. What we lack is their next step: running the checks many times and reporting the spread.</p>
                </div>
              </Fragment>
            )}
            {b.key === 'different' && (
              <Fragment>
                <p class="bucket__summary">On one side only, where the absence fits the other side's context, so nobody should "fix" it.</p>
                <h4>Developer layer</h4>
                <div class="prose">
                  <p><strong>Pseudomonorepo rules, the shared skills library, the Chroma corpus</strong> exist because of our shape: 27 projects, nested submodules, parallel sessions. In one repo with 280 commits, <code>git log</code> does what the corpus does. <strong>Spec Kit</strong> is different but a candidate overbuild on our side. <strong>Legacy per-harness files</strong> are leftovers, not a choice; their single place for instructions is the better state.</p>
                  <p><strong>Deny-all database rules</strong>, on their side, force every write, an agent's included, through the API: a structural guardrail where we would use a prose rule.</p>
                </div>
                <h4>In-product layer</h4>
                <div class="prose">
                  <p><strong>Firm-private submodules</strong> fence each client's inputs and outputs in its own repo. Their customer content lives in the app repo, which is fine for one customer.</p>
                </div>
              </Fragment>
            )}
            {b.key === 'missing' && (
