                <p class="bucket__summary">On one side only, where the other would benefit. In the dev layer every gap is theirs; in the product layer the gaps run mostly toward us.</p>
                <h4>Developer layer (all on their side)</h4>
                <div class="prose">
                  <p><strong>A repo-wide instruction file.</strong> Nothing maps the API routes, libraries or the 23 ordered front-end scripts, and nothing says the old 12,897-line script is dead. With 220 agent-trailered commits, the cost of a cold start recurs. <strong>CI:</strong> their dev gates run only when remembered. <strong>A changelog</strong>, low priority: commit bodies are their only running dev memory, and one commit folded 16 days of work done "outside git".</p>
                </div>
                <h4>In-product layer</h4>
                <div class="prose">
                  <p><strong>On our side, the main lesson.</strong> A simulator plays 18 personas, from <code>native-phd</code> down to <code>stalled</code>, through a real exam and reports a matrix, the correlation with tier, a standard error per candidate and a resolution floor. A gate replay measures how often a decision flips on identical input (<q>flip rate 2% (1/63)</q>). Preflights check that a persona plays its brief and run the shipped prompt against the live model: <q>A result here is a statement about production.</q> None of this exists for memos.</p>
                  <p><strong>On their side, an exception.</strong> <code>/exam-build</code> and <code>/studio</code> implement the same flow twice with no declared source of truth. augment-it wrote that rule at the point of use: <q>the SKILL.md is the source of truth, this slab is its always-loaded condensation; update together</q>.</p>
                </div>
              </Fragment>
            )}
